WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.315
It answers questions in Uzbek. And it runs on a laptop.

00:00:05.081 --> 00:00:08.243
And sometimes, it’s wrong. You’ll see that too.

00:00:09.398 --> 00:00:16.553
Meet OMZU, an assistant for Uzbek, with a small language model trained on Kaggle. It isn’t deployed.

00:00:17.284 --> 00:00:26.199
A model this small shouldn’t guess live facts. Weather? Exchange rates? Those come from tools, and the answer names its source.

00:00:26.945 --> 00:00:34.584
Facts are searched in two hundred and thirty-one thousand Uzbek Wikipedia articles. Let’s ask: who was Ulugh Beg?

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:43.420
The words are shortened to their roots, and looked up in an index. The best passages come back. Are they really about the question? Only those go to the model.

00:00:48.555 --> 00:00:53.198
Inside the index, every word keeps a list of the passages it appears in.

00:00:53.248 --> 00:00:58.841
Run through the lists, and only some passages meet in all of them. Those get a score.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:08.502
So here’s the path of one question. About weather, rates or sums? A tool answers, and the model isn’t used at all.

00:01:08.552 --> 00:01:16.707
Anything else is searched. The best passages are checked against the question, and only then does the model write an answer from them.

00:01:18.301 --> 00:01:26.110
And when nothing reliable is found, it says so. Asked for a neighbour’s phone number, it answers that it has no certain information.

00:01:26.691 --> 00:01:37.548
Now, the model itself. Uzbek and English text is cleaned, and converted to one alphabet. Then a tokenizer is trained on it, with thirty-two thousand pieces.

00:01:37.598 --> 00:01:43.030
The model is pretrained on Kaggle graphics cards, then fine-tuned on questions and answers.

00:01:44.487 --> 00:01:50.823
And it doesn’t see letters. This question becomes five pieces, and each piece is a number.

00:01:50.873 --> 00:01:57.410
A long Uzbek word, “from our books”, needs only two: the root, and everything joined to it.

00:01:58.216 --> 00:02:04.255
About one hundred and ten million parameters, in twelve layers. A word climbs through all of them.

00:02:04.305 --> 00:02:09.604
A cache keeps the earlier tokens, so each new word doesn’t repeat the work.

00:02:11.436 --> 00:02:13.832
And now, the promised mistake.

00:02:14.742 --> 00:02:19.550
Asked what the Silk Road was, it describes a radio station. With the same name.

00:02:20.335 --> 00:02:31.099
Why? These are the real scores. First the search matches the text. Then it multiplies the score for a matching title. And all four titles match equally well.

00:02:31.149 --> 00:02:35.951
The radio station wins, by one point six. That’s the whole mistake.

00:02:36.606 --> 00:02:42.256
So how good is it? It was tested with fifty-two questions, and got thirty-six right.

00:02:43.216 --> 00:02:48.574
But the test only looks for key words. It even counted the radio station as correct.

00:02:48.624 --> 00:02:52.633
So the real score is lower. And now you know exactly why.
